= 13
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=13}

**Natural broadening is about $8\times10^2$ times narrower than the thermal Doppler width and is negligible for this comparison.** The supplied <Doppler broadening> formula is a half width at half maximum. Using the integer molecular mass $m=28m_{\rm u}$,
$$
\nu_0=\frac c\lambda=6.97\times10^{14}\ \mathrm{Hz},\qquad
\Delta\nu_{\rm D,HWHM}=\frac1\lambda\sqrt{\frac{2k_BT\ln2}{m}}=1.06\times10^9\ \mathrm{Hz}.
$$
For an excited-state population lifetime $\tau$, the radiating amplitude decays as $e^{-t/(2\tau)}$. Its <Lorentzian line shape> has <natural linewidth>
$$
\Delta\nu_{\rm nat,HWHM}=\frac{1}{4\pi\tau}=1.33\times10^6\ \mathrm{Hz}.
$$
Equivalently, compare full widths $2.11\ \mathrm{GHz}$ and $2.65\ \mathrm{MHz}$. Using the same linewidth convention in both expressions matters more than the small isotope-mass correction. This estimate assumes that the lower state's lifetime is long and leaves any <pressure broadening> as a separate contribution.

= 14
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=14}

**The Stokes vibrational band has O, Q and S branches**, with $\Delta N=-2,0,+2$, respectively. Here $N$ denotes end-over-end <rotational angular momentum>; treating electronic fine structure as unresolved gives the usual elementary rotor calculation. The absolute incident <wavenumber> is
$$
\widetilde\nu_L=\frac{10^7}{1064}=9398.496\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1},
$$
and energy conservation for <Stokes Raman scattering> gives
$$
\widetilde\nu_s=\widetilde\nu_L-1580-B\{N'(N'+1)-N(N+1)\}.
$$
For the actual $^{16}\mathrm O_2$ ground state, only odd $N$ have nonzero nuclear-spin statistical weight. Thus the lowest initial levels are $N=3$ for O and $N=1$ for Q and S. Ignoring the small change in $B$ upon vibration, the lines are:

|| Branch
|| Lowest transition
|| Rotational energy gained, in $B$
|| Absolute scattered wavenumber, $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$
| O
| $3\to1$
| $-10B$
| **7832.5**
| Q
| $1\to1$
| $0$
| **7818.5**
| S
| $1\to3$
| $10B$
| **7804.5**

The odd-$N$ restriction follows from the zero nuclear spin and exchange symmetry of $^{16}\mathrm O_2$; the electronic spin splits each such $N$ into several $J$ levels without changing this simplified branch-centre calculation.<O2 A-band line parameters to support atmospheric remote sensing Part II by David A. Long and others>[Section 3, manuscript page 4: only odd lower-state N has nonzero statistical weight for oxygen-16 and oxygen-18]{ref}

If the phrase simplest approximation is instead taken to discard nuclear-spin statistics as well, a fictitious unrestricted rotor gives O $2\to0$ at $7826.9\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and S $0\to2$ at $7810.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$, with Q still at $7818.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$. Those even-$N$ lines are not physical ground-state $^{16}\mathrm O_2$ lines.

= 15
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**The terms are $^3\Sigma_g^-$, $^1\Delta_g$ and $^1\Sigma_g^+$.** Closed <molecular orbital> shells contribute no net <spin angular momentum> or axial orbital projection, so only the two equivalent $\pi_g$ electrons need be coupled. Their individual projections are $\lambda=\pm1$.

Putting both electrons in the same projection gives $|\Lambda|=2$ and forces opposite spins by the <Pauli exclusion principle>, yielding $^1\Delta_g$. With one electron in each projection, the spin-adapted symmetric and antisymmetric spatial combinations give a singlet $\Sigma$ and a triplet $\Sigma$. Their reflection parities are $+$ and $-$, respectively. Both open-shell electrons are gerade, so their product is gerade. The dimensions $3+2+1=6$ exhaust the $\binom42=6$ microstates. By <Hund's rules>, the triplet is the lowest term, and $^1\Delta_g$ is the lower singlet.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**The terms are $^1\Sigma_u^+$, $^1\Sigma_u^-$, $^1\Delta_u$, $^3\Sigma_u^+$, $^3\Sigma_u^-$ and $^3\Delta_u$.** The active configuration is $\pi_u^3\pi_g^3$. Each nearly filled $\pi$ shell has the angular-momentum possibilities of one hole, so coupling two distinguishable hole sets gives $\Lambda=0$ or $|\Lambda|=2$ with $S=0$ or $1$.

For $\Lambda=0$, the two arrangements of opposite projections combine with either reflection parity, hence both $\Sigma^+$ and $\Sigma^-$. Unlike the equivalent-electron case in part (a), neither spatial combination is excluded for either spin: the holes belong to different orbital shells. The inversion parity is $u^3g^3=u$. Counting degeneracies gives $1+1+2+3+3+6=16$, equal to the $4\times4$ choices of the two holes.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**Only $^1\Delta_g\to{}^1\Delta_u$ is electric-dipole allowed within the listed terms.** For a <diatomic molecule>, the <electric dipole selection rule> requires $\Delta S=0$, $g\leftrightarrow u$, and $\Delta\Lambda=0,\pm1$. The lower singlet of part (a) is $^1\Delta_g$. Every candidate from the same configuration fails the inversion rule, while the $^1\Sigma_u^{\pm}$ terms of part (b) require $|\Delta\Lambda|=2$. Triplet destinations fail the spin rule. The surviving $\Delta\to\Delta$ transition uses the axial dipole component, so it is a parallel electronic transition. Weak spin-orbit or other intensity-borrowing transitions are outside this leading electric-dipole classification.

= 16
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For positive $\Delta_o\gg B$, expand the common radical as
$$
\sqrt{\Delta_o^2+18B\Delta_o+225B^2}
=\Delta_o+9B+\frac{72B^2}{\Delta_o}+O\!\left(\frac{B^3}{\Delta_o^2}\right).
$$
Substitution into the stated <crystal field theory> energies gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
E_0&=-\frac45\Delta_o+3B-\frac{36B^2}{\Delta_o}+\cdots,\\
E_1&=\frac15\Delta_o,\\
E_2&=\frac15\Delta_o+12B+\frac{36B^2}{\Delta_o}+\cdots,\\
E_3&=\frac65\Delta_o.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The leading straight-line asymptotes retain the terms linear in $\Delta_o$ and constant in $B$. The $B^2/\Delta_o$ terms show from which side the interacting $T_{1g}$ levels approach them.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $B\gg\Delta_o$, the corresponding <asymptotic expansion> is
$$
\sqrt{225B^2+18B\Delta_o+\Delta_o^2}
=15B+\frac35\Delta_o+\frac{8\Delta_o^2}{375B}+\cdots.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
E_0&=-\frac35\Delta_o-\frac{4\Delta_o^2}{375B}+\cdots,\\
E_1&=\frac15\Delta_o,\\
E_2&=15B+\frac{4\Delta_o^2}{375B}+\cdots,\\
E_3&=\frac65\Delta_o.
\end{aligned}}
$$
At zero field, $E_0,E_1,E_3$ converge to the $^3F$ atomic term and $E_2$ to $^3P$ at $15B$. The $^3T_{1g}(P)$ branch therefore has zero initial slope; its high-field slope is not zero.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**The descending line is $^3T_{1g}(F)$; the low rising line is $^3T_{2g}$; the steep line is $^3A_{2g}$; and the branch starting at $15B$ is $^3T_{1g}(P)$.** Their high-field slopes, in the same order, are
$$
\boxed{-\frac45,\quad\frac15,\quad\frac65,\quad\frac15.}
$$
These are slopes of the supplied absolute term energies relative to their chosen reference, not slopes of transition energies measured upward from the ground state. The two $T_{1g}$ states share a <symmetry> and mix, whereas the crossing of $A_{2g}$ and the upper $T_{1g}$ branch is allowed because they have different <irreducible representations>.

\Image[paper-2a-orgel.png]
{title=Triplet crystal-field energies and their strong-field slopes}

